<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests
    Unit and service-level tests for the Gateway service, the sole ingress and composition root of
    the PowerFramework .NET 10 decomposition. This project is the compilation unit for the test
    classes in this folder and the only producer of Gateway's coverage report.

    HAND-AUTHORED ON PURPOSE. This file was not scaffolded from a template and must not be
    regenerated from one. `dotnet new xunit3` does not exist in SDK 10.0.302, and `dotnet new xunit`
    scaffolds the xunit v2 line together with an older test SDK and an older coverage collector.
    Templating is precisely how the wrong package versions get in, so the XML below is written by
    hand and kept deliberately small.

    WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DECLARE. Everything listed below arrives by MSBuild
    directory walk-up from the repository root and applies to test projects exactly as it does to
    application projects. Restating any of it here would be duplication that silently drifts:
      * Directory.Build.props     TargetFramework net10.0, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings,
                                  TreatWarningsAsErrors, .NET analyzers, central package
                                  management, deterministic build, shared assembly metadata.
      * Directory.Packages.props  every package version.
      * global.json               the SDK pin and rollForward latestFeature. The version is read
                                  there rather than copied here, so this comment cannot go stale
                                  the way it once did.

    Consequence worth internalising rather than rediscovering: TreatWarningsAsErrors is inherited
    here too, so a warning in any test file in this folder is a build failure. Fix the warning in
    the test file. Do not add a NoWarn list, do not disable nullable, do not set
    GenerateDocumentationFile, and do not widen the root .editorconfig suppressions from here.

    Solution membership: this project is enumerated by services/gateway-service/gateway-service.slnx
    and by the repository-root PowerFramework.slnx. Because that per-service .slnx is the only
    solution file in services/gateway-service, a bare `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build` or
    `dotnet test` run from that directory resolves it automatically, which is what lets the
    documented per-service command run verbatim. Keep this file's name and directory exactly as
    those solutions declare them, and never add a .slnf filter - because a filter's project list has
    to be kept in exact agreement with the solution BY HAND and every drift fails hard. This note
    used to say such a filter fails with MSB4014 over a .slnx in all three verbs; that was measured
    false on the pinned SDK, and docs/BUILD.md section 2 carries the measurement.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      MANDATORY, NOT STYLISTIC, and verified against the packages themselves rather than assumed.
      xunit.v3 ships its own in-process runner and generates the test assembly's entry point, so a
      v3 test project has to build as an executable. It enforces that with a build-time Error in
      xunit.v3.core.mtp-v1's targets, which fails the build outright when OutputType is anything
      other than Exe (and, separately, when UseAppHost is not true).

      Declared here explicitly on purpose. Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk's own targets currently also force
      OutputType to Exe, so the guard above would be satisfied even by a project that stayed silent.
      Relying on that is fragile: it makes a build-breaking requirement invisible, and it would stop
      holding the moment the test host reference changes or the project moves to running under
      Microsoft Testing Platform directly. State the requirement where it applies. Do not tidy it
      away on the grounds that the build still passes without it.
    -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>

    <!-- A test project is never packed or shipped. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Versions are intentionally absent. ManagePackageVersionsCentrally is enabled at the repository
    root, so Directory.Packages.props is the single source of truth and every reference here stays
    versionless. Adding a Version attribute fights central package management and fails the build
    (NU1008).

    Exactly five packages, and no others. Anything absent from Directory.Packages.props has no
    resolvable version and fails restore (NU1010). That is the mechanism by which the deliberately
    excluded assertion, mocking, fixture, container, logging, telemetry and standalone health-check
    packages stay out: health-check registration already ships in the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App
    shared framework, and the Gateway service uses only those shared-framework abstractions.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Test host. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />

    <!-- The test framework itself. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />

    <!--
      The VSTest adapter, and load-bearing rather than redundant with xunit.v3: without it
      `dotnet test` discovers nothing at all, which reads as a green run with no tests instead of
      an error.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <!--
      Load-bearing for the coverage gate. This project is the only source of
      coverage.cobertura.xml for the Gateway service, and that report is what the 80 percent
      line-coverage-per-service gate is measured from. coverlet.collector is what emits it under
      `dotnet test` with XPlat Code Coverage collection. Drop the package and no report is produced
      at all, so the gate cannot be evaluated, which is a worse outcome than a low number because
      it looks like success.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>

    <!--
      In-process host for the service-level tests that sit alongside the unit tests. Supplies
      WebApplicationFactory over the Gateway entry point, and brings the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App
      framework reference this test project needs in order to consume it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one project reference: the system under test.

    No reference to any other service (DataServices, Persistence or Security). Every service must
    restore, build and test independently from a clean checkout with no sibling service present,
    and a cross-service reference would break that outright. No reference to any deferred-service
    project either: none exists, and none may be created in this phase.

    No direct reference to the shared libraries or to the contracts project. They arrive
    transitively through the Gateway reference, which is where that coupling belongs, and naming
    them here would only duplicate the graph.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../PowerFramework.Gateway/PowerFramework.Gateway.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
